W:
W:Oh, Danny Boy, the pipes, the pipes are calling
W:From glen to glen and down the mountainside
W:The summer’s gone and all the flowers are dying
W:'Tis you, it’s you must go, and I must bide
W:
W:But come ye back when summer’s in the meadow
W:Or when the valley’s hushed and white with snow
W:'Tis I’ll be here in sunshine or in shadow
W:Oh, Danny Boy, oh Danny Boy, I love you so!
W:
W:If you should come, and all the flowers are dying
W:And I am dead, as dead I well may be
W:You’ll come and find the placе where I am lying
W:And kneel and say an Ave thеre for me
W:
W:And I will hear, though soft you tread above me
W:And all my grave shall warmer, sweeter be
W:And you will fall and tell me that you love me
W:And I will rest in peace until you come to me
W:
W:If I should live, and you should die for Ireland
W:Let not your passing thoughts be all for me
W:But say a prayer that God will save our sireland
W:I know He will, and He will set her free!
W:
W:And I will take that pike that you held dearest
W:And strike a blow, though weak that blow may be
W:And it will serve the cause which you died, my love
W:And I will live in peace until you come for me
W:
W:And I shall live in peace until God comes for me!